# Worked solutions, chapter 2 (time response analysis), part 2: 2.54 - 2.98
S = {
"2.54": ("B", "System type = number of integrators in the loop.", "The type is the number of poles at the origin of the *open-loop* transfer function $G(s)H(s)$, and the static error constants ($K_p,K_v,K_a$) follow from it: **(b)**."),
"2.55": ("B", "$t_s(2\\%)=4/(\\xi\\omega_n)$.", "$\\omega_n=5,\\ 2\\xi\\omega_n=5\\Rightarrow\\xi=0.5$, so $\\xi\\omega_n=2.5$.\n$t_s=\\dfrac4{2.5}=1.6$ s: **(b)**."),
"2.56": ("D", "Transient specifications for a step input.", "The standard transient measures are maximum overshoot, delay time, rise time, peak time and settling time. Overall gain is steady-state, and undershoot and fall time are not standard specifications.\n\nSo 1, 4 and 5: **(d)**."),
"2.57": ("B", "Type of the open-loop function $GH$.", "$GH=\\dfrac4{s(s+3)}\\cdot\\dfrac1s=\\dfrac{4}{s^2(s+3)}$: two poles at the origin, so type 2: **(b)**."),
"2.58": ("C", "$\\xi=1$ gives a repeated real pole.", "$s=-\\omega_n$ twice, so the poles are equal, negative and real: **(c)**."),
"2.59": (None, "Order reduction by neglecting a fast pole.", "Same question as 2.8. Removing the non-dominant pole removes a small lag and tends to give a smaller step rise time, but the options say \"for any input\" and rise time is only defined for a step, so the wording is ambiguous and no answer key has been set."),
"2.60": ("A", "DC gain is $G(0)$.", "$G(0)=\\dfrac{5}{5\\cdot4}=0.25$: **(a)**."),
"2.61": ("C", "Sensitivity $S_K^T=\\dfrac KT\\dfrac{dT}{dK}$.", "$\\dfrac{dT}{dK}=\\dfrac{2(3+4K)-4(1+2K)}{(3+4K)^2}=\\dfrac{2}{(3+4K)^2}$.\n$S=\\dfrac{K(3+4K)}{1+2K}\\cdot\\dfrac2{(3+4K)^2}=\\dfrac{2K}{(1+2K)(3+4K)}=\\dfrac{2K}{3+10K+8K^2}$: **(c)**."),
"2.62": ("C", "From overshoot get $\\xi$; then $t_p=\\pi/\\omega_d$.", "$M_p=0.3\\Rightarrow\\xi=\\dfrac{\\ln(1/0.3)}{\\sqrt{\\pi^2+\\ln^2(1/0.3)}}=\\dfrac{1.204}{3.364}=0.358$.\n$\\omega_d=10\\sqrt{1-0.358^2}=9.34$ rad/s.\n$t_p=\\dfrac\\pi{9.34}=0.336$ s: **(c)**."),
"2.63": ("D", "Overshoot grows as $\\xi$ falls.", "Here $2\\xi\\omega_n=2$, so $\\xi=1/\\omega_n$: $0.33,\\ 0.25,\\ 0.2,\\ 0.167$ for $\\omega_n=3,4,5,6$.\nThe smallest damping ratio gives the largest overshoot: $\\dfrac{36}{s^2+2s+36}$, **(d)**."),
"2.64": ("D", "Closed-loop poles: $1+G=0$.", "$(s+2)^2+1=0\\Rightarrow s^2+4s+5=0\\Rightarrow s=-2\\pm j1$: **(d)**."),
"2.65": ("C", "$t_s=4/(\\xi\\omega_n)$.", "$\\omega_n=10$ and $2\\xi\\omega_n=10\\Rightarrow\\xi=0.5$, so $\\xi\\omega_n=5$.\n$t_s=\\dfrac45=0.8$ s: **(c)**."),
"2.66": ("C", "DC gain $=G(0)$, step error $=1-G(0)$ for a closed-loop transfer function.", "$G(0)=1$. Treating $G(s)$ as the system transfer function, the final value of the step response is 1, so the steady-state error is $1-1=0$.\n\nDC gain 1 and error 0: **(c)**."),
"2.67": ("D", "Normalise to standard form.", "$\\ddot x+5\\dot x+7x=7y$: $\\omega_n^2=7\\Rightarrow\\omega_n=\\sqrt7=2.65$ rad/s.\n$2\\xi\\omega_n=5\\Rightarrow\\xi=\\dfrac5{2\\sqrt7}=0.945\\approx0.94$: **(d)**."),
"2.68": ("D", "Zero-input vs zero-state response.", "(I) is false: the zero-*input* response is the response to the initial conditions with the input set to zero, not with zero initial conditions.\n(II) is true: the zero-*state* response is the response to the input with zero initial conditions.\n\n**(d)**."),
"2.69": ("A", "$\\omega_d=\\omega_n\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}$.", "With $\\xi=0$, $\\omega_d=\\omega_n$: **(a)**."),
"2.70": ("B", "Type 0: $e_{ss}=\\dfrac1{1+K_p}$.", "$K_p=G(0)=\\dfrac{12K}{14\\cdot18}=\\dfrac K{21}$.\n$e_{ss}=\\dfrac1{1+K_p}=0.1\\Rightarrow K_p=9\\Rightarrow K=189$: **(b)**."),
"2.71": ("B", "Match $s^2+2\\xi\\omega_ns+\\omega_n^2$.", "$s^2+10s+K$: $2\\xi\\omega_n=10$ with $\\xi=0.5$ gives $\\omega_n=10$.\n$K=\\omega_n^2=100$: **(b)**."),
"2.72": ("A", "Impulse response $\\dfrac{\\omega_n}{\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}}e^{-\\xi\\omega_nt}\\sin\\omega_dt$.", "Reading off: $\\xi\\omega_n=0.8$ and $\\omega_d=0.6$.\n$\\omega_n=\\sqrt{0.8^2+0.6^2}=1$ rad/s and $\\xi=0.8$: **(a)**."),
"2.73": ("C", "$t_s(2\\%)=4/(\\xi\\omega_n)$.", "$\\omega_n=5,\\ \\xi=0.5\\Rightarrow\\xi\\omega_n=2.5$.\n$t_s=\\dfrac4{2.5}=1.6$ s: **(c)**."),
"2.74": ("D", "Definition of settling time.", "The time for the response to reach and stay within a specified percentage of its final value is the settling time: **(d)**."),
"2.75": ("B", "$t_r\\cdot BW\\approx$ constant.", "For a low-pass system $t_r\\approx\\dfrac{0.35}{f_H}$, so rise time is inversely proportional to bandwidth: **(b)**."),
"2.76": ("C", "Low-pass first-order system.", "DC gain $=G(0)=1$. As $s\\to\\infty$, $\\dfrac1{2s+1}\\to0$: **(c)**."),
"2.77": ("A", "$t_s=4/(\\xi\\omega_n)$.", "$\\omega_n=10,\\ 2\\xi\\omega_n=10\\Rightarrow\\xi\\omega_n=5$.\n$t_s=\\dfrac45=0.8$ s: **(a)**."),
"2.78": ("C", "Overdamped step response with poles $-10$ and $-60$.", "The poles give $(s+10)(s+60)=s^2+70s+600$, and the DC gain is 1 (steady value 1).\n$\\omega_n=\\sqrt{600}=24.5$ rad/s.\n$\\xi=\\dfrac{70}{2\\cdot24.5}=1.43$: **(c)**."),
"2.79": ("C", "$t_p=\\pi/\\omega_d$.", "$s^2+6s+25$: $\\omega_n=5,\\ \\xi=0.6,\\ \\omega_d=5\\sqrt{1-0.36}=4$.\n$t_p=\\dfrac\\pi4=0.785$ s: **(c)**."),
"2.80": ("A", "Recover $G$ from $T$: $G=\\dfrac T{1-T}$.", "$G(s)=\\dfrac{\\omega_n^2}{s^2+2\\xi\\omega_ns}=\\dfrac{\\omega_n^2}{s(s+2\\xi\\omega_n)}$.\n$K_v=\\lim_{s\\to0}sG=\\dfrac{\\omega_n^2}{2\\xi\\omega_n}=\\dfrac{\\omega_n}{2\\xi}$: **(a)**."),
"2.81": ("C", "$\\xi=1$: repeated real pole.", "Both poles are at $-\\omega_n$: equal, negative and real: **(c)**."),
"2.82": ("C", "Maximum overshoot definition.", "The largest deviation of the output from the final value during the transient period is the peak (maximum) overshoot: **(c)**."),
"2.83": (None, "Error constants of a type-1 system.", "For a type-1 unity-feedback system $K_p=\\infty$ (not zero), $K_v$ is finite, $K_a=0$, and the step error is $\\dfrac1{1+K_p}=0$ (not 1).\nSo only statement 2 is correct. That is not offered in the options, so the question as transcribed has an error and no answer key has been set."),
"2.84": (None, "Steady-state error with non-unity feedback.", "With an integrator in $G$, the output settles at $c_{ss}=1/K$ for a unit step, so $e_{ss}=\\left|1-\\dfrac1K\\right|<0.05$ gives $0.9524<K<1.0526$.\nBoth $0.953$ and $1.050$ satisfy this, so the question has two valid options and no answer key has been set."),
"2.85": (None, "Settling time from the dominant pole.", "The poles are at $-10$ and $-100$, so the slow pole dominates with $\\tau=0.1$ s. The 2% settling time is about $4\\tau=0.4$ s (exact calculation: $0.40$ s).\nNone of the options (20, 2, 0.2, 0.02 s) matches, so no answer key has been set."),
"2.86": ("C", "Damping ratio from the characteristic equation.", "$2s^2+6s+6=0\\Rightarrow s^2+3s+3=0$: $\\omega_n=\\sqrt3$ and $\\xi=\\dfrac3{2\\sqrt3}=0.866<1$.\nThe system is underdamped: **(c)**."),
"2.87": ("A", "Error constants from the number of integrators.", "$G$ has two poles at the origin: type 2, so $K_p=\\infty$ and $K_v=\\infty$.\n$K_a=\\lim_{s\\to0}s^2G=\\dfrac{2K}{12}=\\dfrac K6$.\n\n**(a)**."),
"2.88": ("B", "Definition of settling time.", "Settling time is the time for the response to settle within a tolerance band (2% or 5%) of the *final value*: **(b)**."),
"2.89": ("D", "Underdamped means complex poles.", "$s^2+as+K=0$ has complex roots when $a^2-4K<0$, i.e. $K>\\dfrac{a^2}{4}$: **(d)**."),
"2.90": ("B", "$t_s=4/(\\xi\\omega_n)$.", "$s^2+6s+25$: $\\omega_n=5$, $\\xi\\omega_n=3$.\n$t_s=\\dfrac43=1.33$ s: **(b)**."),
"2.91": (None, "First-order step response $c(t)=\\dfrac Ka(1-e^{-at})$.", "The figure gives a final value of 5 and a time constant $\\tau=1/a=0.1$ s, so $a=10$ and $K=5a=50$ (equal to the initial slope of 50).\nThe pair $(K,a)=(50,10)$ is not among the options, so the question as transcribed has an error and no answer key has been set."),
"2.92": ("A", "Add an integrator to remove the step error.", "Type 0: $e_{ss}=\\dfrac1{1+K_p}=0.2\\Rightarrow K_p=4$.\nAn integrator ($1/s$) makes the system type 1, so the step error vanishes.\nThe new $K_v=K_p\\cdot1=4$, so the ramp error is $\\dfrac1{K_v}=0.25$: **(a)**."),
"2.93": ("C", "Step response is the integral of the impulse response.", "$G(s)=\\dfrac{16}{s+2}-\\dfrac8{s+1}$.\n$C(s)=\\dfrac{G}{s}=\\dfrac8s-\\dfrac8{s+2}-\\dfrac8s+\\dfrac8{s+1}=\\dfrac8{s+1}-\\dfrac8{s+2}$.\n$c(t)=8e^{-t}-8e^{-2t}$: **(c)**."),
"2.94": ("A", "Partial fractions.", "$Y(s)=\\dfrac5{s(s+1)(s+2)}=\\dfrac{5/2}{s}-\\dfrac5{s+1}+\\dfrac{5/2}{s+2}$.\n$y(t)=\\left[\\dfrac52-5e^{-t}+\\dfrac52e^{-2t}\\right]u(t)$: **(a)**."),
"2.95": ("C", "Type 2: zero error for step and ramp.", "A type-2 system has $K_p=K_v=\\infty$, so both the step and ramp errors are zero: **(c)**."),
"2.96": ("D", "Parabolic input.", "1. A parabola ($t^2/2$) is one degree faster than a ramp: true.\n2. The unit parabolic function is $t^2/2$ for $t>0$: true.\n3. $\\mathcal L\\{t^2/2\\}=\\dfrac1{s^3}$: true.\n\nAll three: **(d)**."),
"2.97": ("A", "First-order step response.", "$C(s)=\\dfrac1{s(1+\\tau s)}=\\dfrac1s-\\dfrac{\\tau}{1+\\tau s}$.\n$c(t)=1-e^{-t/\\tau}$: **(a)**."),
"2.98": ("B", "Step response vs impulse response.", "1. The output due to a unit step: true.\n2. A running *sum* applies to discrete-time systems, so for a continuous system it is false.\n3. The running integral of the impulse response for a continuous-time system: true.\n\n1 and 3 only: **(b)**."),
}
